      "id": "atom-1",
      "text": "The sky has turned orange to blood red in several parts of the United States.",
      "sourceText": "The sky has turned orange to blood red in several parts of the United States.",
      "start": 0,
      "end": 77,
      "role": "CORE"
    }
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "atomId": "atom-1",
      "spans": [
        {
          "id": "doc-7d4923c19c25::sentence-7",
          "documentId": "doc-7d4923c19c25",
          "text": "The scene has been described as apocalyptic, with the sky literally turning dark orange and blood red. ",
          "start": 448,
          "end": 551,
          "contextSpans": [],
          "context": "The scene has been described as apocalyptic, with the sky literally turning dark orange and blood red."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-7d4923c19c25::sentence-43",
          "documentId": "doc-7d4923c19c25",
          "text": "This causes the red and orange colored sky.\n",
          "start": 4836,
          "end": 4880,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-7d4923c19c25::sentence-42",
              "documentId": "doc-7d4923c19c25",
              "text": "If there a sufficient amount of smoke, Mie scattering will dominate over Rayleigh scattering, which is how light is primarily scattered in the atmosphere. ",
              "start": 4681,
              "end": 4836,
              "direction": "PREVIOUS"
            }
          ],
          "context": "If there a sufficient amount of smoke, Mie scattering will dominate over Rayleigh scattering, which is how light is primarily scattered in the atmosphere. This causes the red and orange colored sky."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-4cbdb3ff10fa::sentence-7",
          "documentId": "doc-4cbdb3ff10fa",
          "text": "As hundreds of wildfires tore through Washington, California, and Oregon on Tuesday and Wednesday, their has smoke accumulated in the sky, casting many regions in creepy, apocalyptic shades of orange and red.\n",
          "start": 348,
          "end": 557,
          "contextSpans": [],
          "context": "As hundreds of wildfires tore through Washington, California, and Oregon on Tuesday and Wednesday, their has smoke accumulated in the sky, casting many regions in creepy, apocalyptic shades of orange and red."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-149989fb127e::sentence-10",
          "documentId": "doc-149989fb127e",
          "text": "“Because the sun is low on the horizon, sunlight passes through more air at sunset and sunrise than during the day, when the sun is higher in the sky. More atmosphere means more molecules to scatter the violet and blue light away from your eyes. If the path is long enough, all of the blue and violet light scatters out of your line of sight. The other colors continue on their way to your eyes. This is why sunsets are often yellow, orange, and red.”\n",
          "start": 834,
          "end": 1286,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-149989fb127e::sentence-9",
              "documentId": "doc-149989fb127e",
              "text": "Scattering also explains the colors of the sunrise and sunset, Ackerman says.\n",
              "start": 756,
              "end": 834,
              "direction": "PREVIOUS"
            },
            {
              "id": "doc-149989fb127e::sentence-11",
              "documentId": "doc-149989fb127e",
              "text": "And because red has the longest wavelength of any visible light, the sun is red when it’s on the horizon, where its extremely long path through the atmosphere blocks all other colors.\n",
              "start": 1286,
              "end": 1470,
              "direction": "NEXT"
            }
          ],
          "context": "Scattering also explains the colors of the sunrise and sunset, Ackerman says. “Because the sun is low on the horizon, sunlight passes through more air at sunset and sunrise than during the day, when the sun is higher in the sky. More atmosphere means more molecules to scatter the violet and blue light away from your eyes. If the path is long enough, all of the blue and violet light scatters out of your line of sight. The other colors continue on their way to your eyes. This is why sunsets are often yellow, orange, and red.” And because red has the longest wavelength of any visible light, the sun is red when it’s on the horizon, where its extremely long path through the atmosphere blocks all other colors."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-4cbdb3ff10fa::sentence-16",
          "documentId": "doc-4cbdb3ff10fa",
          "text": "That's a phenomenon called Mie scattering, and it's why skies turn orange and red due to wildfire smoke (it doesn't have to do with the color of the flames themselves).\n",
          "start": 1660,
          "end": 1829,
          "contextSpans": [
            {
              "id": "doc-4cbdb3ff10fa::sentence-15",
              "documentId": "doc-4cbdb3ff10fa",
              "text": "Particles from smoke, meanwhile — like volatile organic compounds, dust, and soot — are much larger than gas molecules, and they scatter light with longer wavelengths. ",
              "start": 1492,
              "end": 1660,
              "direction": "PREVIOUS"
            }
          ],
          "context": "Particles from smoke, meanwhile — like volatile organic compounds, dust, and soot — are much larger than gas molecules, and they scatter light with longer wavelengths. That's a phenomenon called Mie scattering, and it's why skies turn orange and red due to wildfire smoke (it doesn't have to do with the color of the flames themselves)."
        },
        {
          "id": "doc-7d4923c19c25::sentence-40",
          "documentId": "doc-7d4923c19c25",
          "text": "The reason for the sky turning red is the way smoke particles scatter sunlight. ",
          "start": 4475,
          "end": 4555,
          "contextSpans": [],
          "context": "The reason for the sky turning red is the way smoke particles scatter sunlight."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
